<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-longitude-and-time" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Longitude and Time</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        2.7 Navigation
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        2.3 Navigation
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p component="core">
                        Relate longitudes to time at different points on the earth's surface
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        Determine local time on the earth's surface using longitudinal difference
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Sheet title: Longitude and Time (Time Zones)
         Content summary: Longitude and time: 15° = 1 hour; local time at different longitudes; time zones.
         Suggested activity: Learners use a world map to find local times in cities such as London, Dubai and New York when it is 12 noon in Nairobi.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Slider for longitude shows local time relative to Greenwich (15° = 1 hour), with East Africa Time marked. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (d); Essential (c). Lessons 4.1 and 4.2 as subsections. Lesson 4.2 also carries
         Core (g) / Essential (f) (apply) and Core (h) / Essential (g) (role of navigation), and the Curriculum Links axiom.
         Activity: as planning notes (local times in London, Dubai and New York at noon in Nairobi)
         JSXGraph: fig-longitude-time-dial (assets/jsxgraph/longitude-time-dial.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/04-longitude-and-time/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 4.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-local-time-and-longitude">
        <title>Local Time and Longitude</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the earth turns through <m>15^\circ</m> every hour, so <m>1^\circ</m> of longitude is <m>4</m> minutes of time; places to the east are ahead in local time and places to the west behind; Greenwich Mean Time; time zones; the day can change.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the local time at another longitude; find a longitude from a time difference; find the day and time on the other side of the world.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> world times from Nairobi.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners work out that the earth turns <m>15^\circ</m> in an hour and <m>1^\circ</m> in <m>4</m> minutes, use this to find local times, and compare local solar time with clock time zones.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Darken the room. Shine a torch (the sun) on the globe and turn the globe slowly eastwards (anticlockwise seen from above the North Pole). Ask: <q>Which town sees the sun highest first, Mombasa or Kisumu?</q> Then use the interactive in <xref ref="fig-longitude-time-dial"/>.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-noon-in-nairobi">
            <title>Noon in Nairobi</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Take Nairobi to be on longitude <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m> and Kisumu on <m>35^\circ\,\text{E}</m>. <em>Local time</em> at a place is the time by the sun there: it is 12:00 local time when the sun is highest in the sky.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>The earth turns once (<m>360^\circ</m>) in <m>24</m> hours. Through how many degrees does it turn in <m>1</m> hour? In <m>4</m> minutes?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>360 \div 24 = 15^\circ</m> in an hour; <m>15^\circ</m> in <m>60</m> minutes is <m>1^\circ</m> in <m>4</m> minutes.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>When the sun is highest over Nairobi, how long is it until the sun is highest over Kisumu?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Kisumu is <m>2^\circ</m> west of Nairobi, so the sun reaches it <m>2 \times 4 = 8</m> minutes later.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>When it is 12:00 local time on <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m>, find the local times in London (<m>0^\circ</m>), Dubai (<m>55^\circ\,\text{E}</m>) and New York (<m>74^\circ\,\text{W}</m>).</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>London is <m>37^\circ</m> west: <m>148</m> minutes behind, 09:32. Dubai is <m>18^\circ</m> east: <m>72</m> minutes ahead, 13:12. New York is <m>37 + 74 = 111^\circ</m> west: <m>444</m> minutes <m>= 7\,\text{h}\ 24\,\text{min}</m> behind, 04:36.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>In January, clocks in Nairobi show East Africa Time (EAT), which is GMT <m>+ 3</m> hours. Clocks in London show GMT, in Dubai GMT <m>+ 4</m> hours and in New York GMT <m>- 5</m> hours. When Nairobi clocks show 12:00, what do the clocks in the other three cities show? Why are the answers to (c) and (d) different?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>12:00 EAT is 09:00 GMT, so London 09:00, Dubai 13:00 and New York 04:00. Clocks follow time zones that differ by whole hours; local time follows the exact longitude.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To state the link between longitude and local time that learners found in the activity.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (a) gives the two facts used all lesson: <m>15^\circ</m> is <m>1</m> hour and <m>1^\circ</m> is <m>4</m> minutes.</p></li>
                    <li><p>East is ahead (add), west is behind (subtract). Find the difference in longitude first, with the same rule as for positions: same side of Greenwich subtract, opposite sides add.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Time zones are a convention: clocks in a zone all show the same time, which is the local time of one chosen meridian.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-longitude-and-local-time">
            <title>Longitude and Local Time</title>
            <p>The <term>local time</term> at a place is the time by the sun there: 12:00 when the sun is highest. The earth turns eastwards through <m>15^\circ</m> every hour, so the sun reaches places to the east first.</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>360^\circ \amp = 24\ \text{hours}, \amp 15^\circ \amp = 1\ \text{hour}, \amp 1^\circ \amp = 4\ \text{minutes}, \amp 15' \amp = 1\ \text{minute}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>Time difference between two places <m>=</m> difference in longitude (in degrees) <m>\times 4</m> minutes. A place east of another is ahead in local time; a place west of it is behind. <term>Greenwich Mean Time</term> (GMT) is the local time on the Greenwich meridian, <m>0^\circ</m>.</p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-longitude-time-dial">
            <caption>Local time and longitude. Set the time at Greenwich, then move the longitude marker: every <m>15^\circ</m> east adds an hour.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-longitude-time-dial" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:10" source="jsxgraph/longitude-time-dial.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-longitude-time-dial" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:10"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Set the <alert>GMT</alert> slider (steps of <m>15</m> minutes), then move the <alert>longitude</alert> slider (steps of <m>1^\circ</m>). The readout shows the local time at the marker, with <q>+1 day</q> or <q>-1 day</q> when the date is different from Greenwich.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Check: at 09:00 GMT, the local time at Nairobi (<m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m>) is 11:28, and at <m>45^\circ\,\text{E}</m> it is 12:00.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Compare Nairobi's local time with East Africa Time, the local time of the <m>45^\circ\,\text{E}</m> meridian.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <note xml:id="note-time-zones-eat">
            <title>Time Zones and East Africa Time</title>
            <p>If every town kept its own local time, clocks in Nairobi and Mombasa would differ by about <m>11</m> minutes. Instead, most countries use <term>time zones</term>: every clock in a zone shows the same time, usually a whole number of hours ahead of or behind GMT. Kenya, Uganda and Tanzania use East Africa Time (EAT), which is GMT <m>+ 3</m> hours: the local time of the <m>45^\circ\,\text{E}</m> meridian. Nairobi is about <m>8^\circ</m> west of that meridian, so the sun is highest over Nairobi at about 12:33 EAT. In this chapter, <q>local time</q> always means the time by longitude, not the time zone.</p>
        </note>

        <note xml:id="note-international-date-line">
            <title>The International Date Line</title>
            <p>Travel far enough east or west and the time difference reaches <m>12</m> hours, at the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian. The <term>International Date Line</term> runs through the Pacific Ocean, roughly along <m>180^\circ</m>. Crossing it travelling westwards, you add a day; travelling eastwards, you take a day away. The line bends round some island groups so that each country keeps one date.</p>
        </note>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-mombasa-and-kampala">
            <title>Mombasa and Kampala</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Take Mombasa to be on longitude <m>40^\circ\,\text{E}</m> and Kampala on <m>33^\circ\,\text{E}</m>. Find the local time in Kampala when it is 12:00 local time in Mombasa.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Both are east of Greenwich, so the difference in longitude is <m>40^\circ - 33^\circ = 7^\circ</m>, which is <m>7 \times 4 = 28</m> minutes. Kampala is west of Mombasa, so its local time is behind: <m>12{:}00 - 28\,\text{min} = 11{:}32</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-accra-and-nairobi">
            <title>Accra and Nairobi</title>
            <statement>
                <p>It is 15:20 local time in Nairobi (take <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m>). Take Accra to be on the Greenwich meridian, <m>0^\circ</m>. What is the local time in Accra?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Difference in longitude <m>37^\circ</m>, so <m>37 \times 4 = 148\,\text{min} = 2\,\text{h}\ 28\,\text{min}</m>. Accra is west, so behind: <m>15{:}20 - 2{:}28 = 12{:}52</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-finding-a-ships-longitude">
            <title>Finding a Ship's Longitude</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A ship's navigator sees that the local time on board is 05:24 when it is 08:00 GMT. Find the ship's longitude.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The ship's time is behind GMT by <m>8{:}00 - 5{:}24 = 2\,\text{h}\ 36\,\text{min} = 156\,\text{min}</m>. Each degree is <m>4</m> minutes, so the ship is <m>156 \div 4 = 39^\circ</m> from Greenwich. Behind means west: the ship is on longitude <m>39^\circ\,\text{W}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-across-the-pacific">
            <title>Across the Pacific</title>
            <statement>
                <p>It is 05:00 on Wednesday, local time, in Nairobi (<m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m>). Take Honolulu to be on longitude <m>158^\circ\,\text{W}</m>. What are the day and local time in Honolulu?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Nairobi and Honolulu are on opposite sides of Greenwich, so add: <m>37^\circ + 158^\circ = 195^\circ</m>. <m>195 \times 4 = 780\,\text{min} = 13\,\text{h}</m>. Honolulu is west of Nairobi, so it is behind: <m>13</m> hours before 05:00 on Wednesday is 16:00 on Tuesday.</p>
                <p>Counting the other way, eastwards across the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian, Honolulu is <m>165^\circ</m> away and <m>11</m> hours ahead, which also gives 16:00. Crossing the International Date Line (<xref ref="note-international-date-line"/>) eastwards takes a day away, so the day is Tuesday either way.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>36.8</c>. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.</p>
        <p>Give times on the 24-hour clock: type the hours and the minutes in separate boxes, for example 14 and 05 for 14:05.</p>

