<exercise xml:id="ex-day-and-time-across-the-world">
    <!--<title></title>-->
    <stack label="stk-day-and-time-across-the-world" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/04-longitude-and-time/Day-and-time-across-the-world.xml"/>
</exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-local-time-and-longitude">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-garissa-and-kisumu">
                <statement><p>Take Garissa to be on longitude <m>40^\circ\,\text{E}</m> and Kisumu on <m>35^\circ\,\text{E}</m>. It is 09:00 local time in Garissa. What is the local time in Kisumu?</p></statement>
                <answer><p>08:40</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Difference in longitude <m>40^\circ - 35^\circ = 5^\circ</m>, and <m>5 \times 4 = 20</m> minutes of time. Kisumu is west of Garissa, so it is behind: <m>09{:}00 - 20</m> minutes gives 08:40.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-from-greenwich">
                <statement><p>It is 13:00 GMT. Find the local time on longitude <m>75^\circ\,\text{W}</m> and on longitude <m>120^\circ\,\text{E}</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>08:00; 21:00</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>75 \times 4 = 300\,\text{min} = 5\,\text{h}</m> behind: 08:00. <m>120 \times 4 = 480\,\text{min} = 8\,\text{h}</m> ahead: 21:00.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-longitude-mauritius">
                <statement><p>When it is 12:00 GMT, the local time on a ship is 15:48. Find the ship's longitude.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>57^\circ\,\text{E}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The ship is <m>3\,\text{h}\ 48\,\text{min} = 228\,\text{min}</m> ahead, so <m>228 \div 4 = 57^\circ</m> east.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-wellington-and-lima">
                <statement><p>It is 10:00 on Wednesday, local time, in Nairobi (<m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m>). Find the day and local time in Wellington (take <m>175^\circ\,\text{E}</m>) and in Lima (take <m>77^\circ\,\text{W}</m>).</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Wellington: Wednesday 19:12. Lima: Wednesday 02:24.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Wellington is <m>175 - 37 = 138^\circ</m> east: <m>552\,\text{min} = 9\,\text{h}\ 12\,\text{min}</m> ahead, 19:12 on Wednesday. Lima is <m>37 + 77 = 114^\circ</m> west: <m>456\,\text{min} = 7\,\text{h}\ 36\,\text{min}</m> behind, 02:24 on Wednesday.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-friday-night-in-nairobi">
                <statement><p>It is 21:30 on Friday, local time, in Nairobi (<m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m>). Find the day and local time on longitude <m>165^\circ\,\text{E}</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Saturday 06:02</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>165 - 37 = 128^\circ</m> east: <m>512\,\text{min} = 8\,\text{h}\ 32\,\text{min}</m> ahead. <m>21{:}30 + 8{:}32 = 30{:}02</m>, which is past midnight: 06:02 on Saturday.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-two-possible-longitudes">
                <title>Challenge: Two Possible Longitudes</title>
                <statement><p>The local time at a place P differs by <m>3\,\text{h}\ 20\,\text{min}</m> from the local time on longitude <m>25^\circ\,\text{E}</m>. Find the two possible longitudes of P.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>P could be east or west of <m>25^\circ\,\text{E}</m>.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>75^\circ\,\text{E}</m> or <m>25^\circ\,\text{W}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>3\,\text{h}\ 20\,\text{min} = 200\,\text{min}</m>, and <m>200 \div 4 = 50^\circ</m>. East: <m>25^\circ + 50^\circ = 75^\circ\,\text{E}</m>. West: <m>50^\circ - 25^\circ = 25^\circ</m> past Greenwich, so <m>25^\circ\,\text{W}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-local-time-and-longitude">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Activity parts (a) to (c), <xref ref="example-mombasa-and-kampala"/> and <xref ref="example-accra-and-nairobi"/>. Give a card: <q>East is ahead: add. West is behind: subtract. <m>1^\circ</m> = 4 min.</q> Assign <xref ref="ex-garissa-and-kisumu"/> and <xref ref="ex-from-greenwich"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-garissa-and-kisumu"/> to <xref ref="ex-friday-night-in-nairobi"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-two-possible-longitudes"/>. Extension: at what longitude is local time exactly <m>12</m> hours different from Greenwich? (Answer: <m>180^\circ</m>.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Closing question for the class: why does a business in Nairobi that phones a customer in London need to know the time difference?</p>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 4.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-journeys-across-longitudes">
        <title>Journeys Across Longitudes</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the local time on arrival is the departure local time plus the journey time, then plus or minus the time difference between the two longitudes.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find arrival times, journey times and speeds from local times.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> flights and voyages along the equator and parallels; recognise the role of navigation in daily life.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners combine distance, speed and local time for a long voyage along the equator.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Open with a story: a visitor has just arrived in Nairobi and her phone still shows the time at home. How should she reset it, and how could you help her? (Caring for a visitor is part of the value love.) Let groups do parts (a) to (c) before discussing part (d).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-five-days-on-the-equator">
            <title>Five Days on the Equator</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A ship sails due east along the equator from longitude <m>42^\circ\,\text{E}</m> to longitude <m>73^\circ\,\text{E}</m> at <m>15.5\,\text{knots}</m>. It leaves at 08:00 on Monday, local time.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>How far does the ship sail, in nautical miles?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>73^\circ - 42^\circ = 31^\circ = 1860'</m>, so <m>1860\,\text{nm}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>How long does the voyage take?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>1860 \div 15.5 = 120\,\text{h} = 5</m> days.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>What is the time of arrival by the clock of the starting port?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Five days after 08:00 on Monday: 08:00 on Saturday.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>What is the local time on <m>73^\circ\,\text{E}</m> when the ship arrives?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The ship has moved <m>31^\circ</m> east, so local time is <m>31 \times 4 = 124\,\text{min}</m> ahead: 10:04 on Saturday.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>A passenger's watch still shows the time at the starting port. How should she change it?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Forward by <m>2\,\text{h}\ 04\,\text{min}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To set out the three steps for the local time at the end of a journey.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> parts (b), (c) and (d) of the activity are the three steps: journey time, the starting clock on arrival, then the change of local time.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Travelling east, local time gains <m>4</m> minutes for every degree; travelling west, it loses <m>4</m> minutes for every degree.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Return to the key inquiry question of the chapter: how do we use navigation in daily life?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-arrival-local-time">
            <title>Arrival Local Time</title>
            <p>For a journey from one longitude to another:</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="1.">
                    <li><p>Find the journey time from the distance and the speed.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Add it to the departure time: this is the arrival time by the clock of the starting place.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Change to local time at the end: add <m>4</m> minutes for each degree travelled east, or subtract <m>4</m> minutes for each degree travelled west.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\text{arrival local time} = \text{departure local time} + \text{journey time} \pm 4\ \text{min} \times (\text{degrees of longitude travelled})</md>
                with <m>+</m> for a journey east and <m>-</m> for a journey west.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-a-westward-flight">
            <title>A Westward Flight</title>
            <statement>
                <p>An aircraft flies due west along the equator from longitude <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m> to longitude <m>13^\circ\,\text{E}</m> at <m>450\,\text{knots}</m>. It leaves at 07:30 local time. Find the local time when it arrives.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><alert>Step 1: journey time.</alert> Distance <m>(37 - 13) \times 60 = 1440\,\text{nm}</m>. Time <m>1440 \div 450 = 3.2\,\text{h} = 3\,\text{h}\ 12\,\text{min}</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 2: starting clock.</alert> <m>07{:}30 + 3{:}12 = 10{:}42</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 3: local time.</alert> The aircraft travelled <m>24^\circ</m> west, so local time is <m>24 \times 4 = 96\,\text{min} = 1\,\text{h}\ 36\,\text{min}</m> behind: <m>10{:}42 - 1{:}36 = 09{:}06</m>. It arrives at 09:06 local time.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-speed-from-local-times">
            <title>Finding the Speed from Local Times</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A plane leaves longitude <m>0^\circ</m> on the equator at 09:00 local time and flies due east along the equator to longitude <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m>, arriving at 17:58 local time. Find its speed in knots.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The local times differ by <m>17{:}58 - 9{:}00 = 8\,\text{h}\ 58\,\text{min}</m>. Part of this is the change of local time: <m>37 \times 4 = 148\,\text{min} = 2\,\text{h}\ 28\,\text{min}</m>, gained by flying east. So the flight time is <m>8\,\text{h}\ 58\,\text{min} - 2\,\text{h}\ 28\,\text{min} = 6\,\text{h}\ 30\,\text{min} = 6.5\,\text{h}</m>.</p>
                <p>Distance <m>37 \times 60 = 2220\,\text{nm}</m>. Speed <m>2220 \div 6.5 = 341.5\,\text{knots}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <insight xml:id="insight-navigation-around-us">
            <title>Navigation Around Us</title>
            <p>Navigation is finding where you are and how to reach where you want to go. In Kenya it is used every day:</p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p>ships entering the ports of Mombasa and Lamu, and dhows along the coast;</p></li>
                    <li><p>Kenya Airways and other flights planning routes, fuel and arrival times across time zones;</p></li>
                    <li><p>fishermen on Lake Victoria and in the Indian Ocean finding their way back to shore;</p></li>
                    <li><p>phone maps and GPS used by matatu, boda boda and delivery riders;</p></li>
                    <li><p>search and rescue teams giving positions in latitude and longitude;</p></li>
                    <li><p>businesses arranging calls and deliveries with partners in other time zones.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </insight>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Curriculum Links</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>How do we use navigation in daily life? Revisit it with <xref ref="insight-navigation-around-us"/>.</p></li>
                <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Citizenship (global awareness: world times in the activity of <xref ref="subsec-local-time-and-longitude"/>); digital literacy (the interactives, phone GPS positions).</p></li>
                <li><title>Values</title><p>Love: helping a visitor whose phone still shows home time.</p></li>
                <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p component="core">Safety and security; online safety (think before sharing your live location).</p><p component="essential">Online safety (think before sharing your live location).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>36.8</c>. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.</p>
        <p>Give times on the 24-hour clock: type the hours and the minutes in separate boxes, for example 14 and 05 for 14:05.</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-arrival-local-time-after-a-flight">
    <!--<title></title>-->
    <stack label="stk-arrival-local-time-after-a-flight" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/04-longitude-and-time/Arrival-local-time-after-a-flight.xml"/>
</exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-journeys-across-longitudes">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-westward-flight-20e">
                <statement><p>A plane leaves longitude <m>20^\circ\,\text{E}</m> on the equator at 06:00 local time and flies due west along the equator at <m>400\,\text{knots}</m> to longitude <m>10^\circ\,\text{W}</m>. Find the local time when it arrives.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>08:30</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Difference in longitude <m>20^\circ + 10^\circ = 30^\circ</m>, so <m>1800\,\text{nm}</m>. Time <m>1800 \div 400 = 4.5\,\text{h} = 4\,\text{h}\ 30\,\text{min}</m>; starting clock <m>10{:}30</m>. West by <m>30^\circ</m>: <m>120\,\text{min}</m> behind, so 08:30 local time.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-voyage-along-32s">
                <statement><p>A ship sails along the parallel <m>32^\circ\,\text{S}</m> from longitude <m>30^\circ\,\text{E}</m> to longitude <m>115^\circ\,\text{E}</m> at <m>16\,\text{knots}</m>. How long does the voyage take, to the nearest hour?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>270</m> hours</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Difference in longitude <m>85^\circ = 5100'</m>. Distance <m>5100 \cos 32^\circ = 4325.0\,\text{nm}</m>. Time <m>4325.0 \div 16 = 270.3</m>, about <m>270\,\text{h}</m> (just over <m>11</m> days).</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-speed-from-local-times">
                <statement><p>A plane leaves longitude <m>10^\circ\,\text{W}</m> on the equator at 08:00 local time and flies due east along the equator to longitude <m>26^\circ\,\text{E}</m>, arriving at 15:24 local time. Find its speed in knots.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>432\,\text{knots}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Difference in longitude <m>10^\circ + 26^\circ = 36^\circ</m>, so <m>2160\,\text{nm}</m>, and local time gains <m>36 \times 4 = 144\,\text{min} = 2\,\text{h}\ 24\,\text{min}</m>. The local times differ by <m>7\,\text{h}\ 24\,\text{min}</m>, so the flight takes <m>5\,\text{h}</m>. Speed <m>2160 \div 5 = 432\,\text{knots}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-ships-clock">
                <title>Challenge: The Ship's Clock</title>
                <statement><p>A ship sails due west along the equator, covering <m>300\,\text{nm}</m> each day. Its clock is set to local time once a day. By how much is the clock changed each day, and is it put forward or back?</p></statement>
                <hint><p>How many degrees of longitude is <m>300\,\text{nm}</m> along the equator?</p></hint>
                <answer><p>Back <m>20</m> minutes each day</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>300\,\text{nm} = 300' = 5^\circ</m> a day. <m>5 \times 4 = 20</m> minutes. The ship moves west, where local time is behind, so the clock is put back <m>20</m> minutes each day.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-journeys-across-longitudes">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Activity parts (a) to (d) and <xref ref="example-a-westward-flight"/>. Give a card with the three steps of <xref ref="insight-arrival-local-time"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-westward-flight-20e"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-westward-flight-20e"/> to <xref ref="ex-speed-from-local-times"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-ships-clock"/>. Extension: a plane leaves a town on the equator at 08:00 local time, flies due west along the equator for <m>3</m> hours and lands at 10:00 local time. Find its speed. (Answer: by the starting clock it lands at 11:00, so local time is <m>1</m> hour behind: <m>15^\circ</m> of longitude, which is <m>900\,\text{nm}</m> in <m>3</m> hours, <m>300\,\text{knots}</m>.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Closing question for the class: name one way navigation is used in your county, and say what it measures (a position, a distance, a speed or a time).</p>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>
</section>
